News - Scientists warn that the virus mutation may cause another “very very bad” Virus epidemic science
“A worrying issue is that B.1.1.7 will become a major global variant with a higher transmission rate, and it will trigger another very, very bad wave,” the director in charge of the Wellcome Trust Infectious disease expert Jeremy Farrar (Jeremy Farrar) said. Farrar said that given that the virus’s pandemic trajectory in 2020 is quite predictable, “due to the evolution of the virus, I think we are now entering an unpredictable stage.”
Kuchersky said that increasing the spread of the virus is more dangerous than increasing the pathogenicity, because its effect increases exponentially. “If you have something that can kill 1% of people, but a large number of people die, it will cause more deaths than a few people get and kill 2% of people.”
A growing body of evidence shows that mutations in a new variant of SARS-CoV-2 have helped to increase infections in the UK in the past month.
Farrar added that other variants have emerged, especially a variant called 501Y.V2 in South Africa, which is as worrying as B.1.1.7. He said: “In essence, this is a numbers game: the more the virus spreads, the more opportunities for mutants to appear.” In the long run, mutations may appear that threaten the efficacy of the vaccine.
