Six Ways Robotic Automation Benefits CNC Shops…and Their Customers
In the face of increasing competition, CNC manufacturing has been in an ongoing battle to control production costs, improve product quality and meet customer needs.To meet these challenges, CNC shops employ state-of-the-art technology to reduce costs and increase productivity.
Higher Efficiency and Productivity Robots can perform cutting, grinding or milling with higher uptime, producing 47% more parts per hour compared to traditional methods.While the benefits of CNC machine tools are enormous, adding robotic automation to a CNC shop can significantly increase throughput without exceeding budget constraints.
Modern self-contained robotic CNC machine tenders can handle multiple component sizes, IDs and ODs more efficiently than humans.The robot itself is operated using a menu-driven touchscreen HMI, ideal for non-programmers.
The improved labor safety and security robot includes many features to ensure that employees enjoy a high degree of safety while performing core tasks.As an added benefit, implementing bots for specific processes allows humans to prioritize cognitive-oriented tasks.
The tender itself can be installed and operated without professional supervision.Additionally, programming tenders is relatively simple, which speeds up their deployment and redeployment.
Unlike human labor, robots can efficiently serve multiple machine parts.Loading a workpiece into a machine is easily done by a robot, and you can program the robot to load another machine during machining.This practice is time-saving because the two processes are performed simultaneously.
Better contract pricing robots ensure manufacturing consistency on the CNC shop floor.This enables companies to more accurately estimate production duration and associated expenditures, which in turn improves contract pricing.
Last word Robots are very productive, relatively simple to operate, and at the same time economically viable.As a result, robotic automation has gained widespread acceptance in the CNC industry, with more and more CNC shop owners incorporating robots into various manufacturing and production processes.
