Exports Rebound While Imports Decline, More Efforts Are Needed To Keep Foreign Trade Stable
China’s exports increased by 8.2% in April compared with the same month last year, while imports fell 10.2 percent, according to the latest data from the General Administration of Customs, P.R.China. This “export rose and import fell” trend basically formed a reversal with the situation in the first quarter.
According to the data, China’s total volume of foreign trade in April reached 250 million yuan, slightly declined by 0.7% year on year. Among this, exports totaled 1.41 trillion yuan, rose by 8.2%; Imports totaled 1.09 trillion yuan, decreased by 10.2%.
“I thought April wouldn’t be a good month either.”Bai Ming, deputy director of the institute of international market research at the Ministry of Commerce, admitted, “or even if there is some improvement, there will not be such big year-on-year growth.”
Insiders believe that the majority of foreign trade market players should make more efforts in developing emerging markets, transformation and upgrading, and at the same time, they can also “look inside”, and make better use of both domestic and international markets and resources to further enhance the resilience of China’s foreign trade.
