The corrosion resistance of stainless steel depends on a very thin but strong and stable chrome-rich oxide film (protective film) on its surface, which prevent the oxygen atom from permeating.
There are many forms of this kind of surface destruction, and the following are commonly seen in our daily life: 1.
Organic juice attached in the surface of stainless steel, such as melon and vegetables, noodle soup, phlegm, etc. In the presence of water and oxygen, the organic acid forms, which causes corrosion in the metal surface.
Stainless steel surface contains acid, alkali, salt (such as alkali water, limewater splashing when decorate the wall), causes partly corrosion.
In the polluted air (such as the air containing a large amount of sulfide, oxidized carbon, nitric oxide), the condensation water will form sulfuric acid, nitric acid, acetic acid liquid points, causing chemical corrosion.
Then how to avoid the problem?
