A building can collapse when the structure on the ground cannot withstand the load due to various influences such as weakening of ground due to a natural disaster, deterioration of the structure over time, poor construction, poor maintenance, illegal alteration of the purpose of use, illegal extension/reconstruction and poor safety inspection. ·Slope collapse slope
If you are in a safe zone, stay there. Do not use an elevator, or walk down a damaged stairway.
If there is a risk of a gas leak, prevent an explosion by not lighting a match and/or a stove. Use a flashlight instead.
Stay calm and look around to find an evacuation route.
Go to a room with strong walls such as an elevator hall or staircase.
If someone is injured, bring the person to a safe place and administer first aid.
When evacuating, an adult who is familiar with the building shall lead. Everyone must cover their head with a cushion or the like, and move in good order.
If possible, do not move any obstacles on the way out. If you must move an obstacle, be prepared for an additional collapse.
Cover your head and face with a jacket, blanket, newspaper or box to protect yourself fr om falling objects and glass.
If it seems like you will have to stay inside for a while, find food and water fr om a refrigerator. Eat carefully to so that the supply of food will last longer.
If you are stuck inside a pile of debris, move your fingers and toes frequently to promote blood circulation and cover your mouth and nose with a piece of cloth to minimize breathing.
Keep thinking about your loved ones, and look for a chance to survive in order to stay positive and awake.
When you hear the rescue team, react calmly. Do not yell excessively; it is important to conserve your energy.
If you think that there are people nearby, shine the flashlight, yell and/or tap on a pipe to let them know that you are there.
When asking for help, try not to waste your energy and keep your body comfortable.
Tap on a pipe regularly, yell out, or call 119 with your cell phone.
·In the event of a landslide
- You can receive help fr om Seoul Metropolitan Government’s Disaster Mental Health Information Support System (DMHISS). You can call directly, or talk to paramedics and other officers dispatched to the disaster site for counseling.
