To what extent is terroir decisive in the final quality of wines?
Well, back to the terroir theme, the main question that arose was whether and how much terroir can influence the quality of a wine. That is, considering two producers, each located in a geographical area very different from the other, who use the same strains, the same techniques and the same form of production/handling in each stage, at the end of the same year as will be the wines produced by these two producers?
Jumping straight to the conclusion, yes, the wines can have different qualities, as terroir is one of the key factors in wine production.
The fundamental importance of the terroir in the final quality of the wine was at the heart of the struggle for the controlled designation of origin (DOC) in France and Italy, the second and first largest wine producers in the world, respectively. It is not by chance that all French and Italian specialist works evoke the way in which terroir characteristics affect the aromas and taste of wine.
