Even if some people do not understand exactly why a wine has this classification, it is clear that it is something that enhances the quality of the wine. But, anyway, what exactly does it mean and what does the classification of a wine as a DOC impact on?
There were about 10 years of struggle along with other French producers and legislators, until in 1930 the law was enacted that established the status of winemakers in the Châteauneuf-du-Pape region, with the controlled designation of origin. Thus, the DOC terminology started to be used exclusively in the wines produced in Châteauneuf-du-Pape, until today considered one of the most prestigious wine regions in the world.
Subsequently, the “guaranteed” terminology was introduced for wines produced under the strict criteria established in the statutes, creating the term DOCG, that is, Denomination of Controlled and Guaranteed Origin. The producers of Brunello di Montalcino, in the Italian region of Tuscany, were among the first to be able to use DOCG terminology in their wines.
