Continuing with our journey through Brazilian capitals, we stop in Teresina, the capital of the small state of Piauí, the first capital in the northeast of the country in our series on Brazilian capitals. One of the great curiosities of Teresina is the fact that it is the only northeastern capital without beaches.
Teresina is also known as Cidade Verde, because thanks to its urban planning, it began to be called that by the Maranhão writer Coelho Neto, because of its avenues interspersed with trees, in addition to having large and beautiful urban parks. An excellent way to appreciate this city covered in green is through the 95-meter high viewpoint installed on the Estaiada Bridge, the biggest postcard of Teresina.
And if the municipal park stirs up curiosity for legacies from the remote past, about 400 kilometers from Teresina is the Serra da Capivara National Park, famous for its rock carvings that date back to the prehistoric period and are the oldest on the continent. Amidst beautiful rock formations dating back to the pre-Columbian era, Pedra Furada stands out, one of the most photographed spots in the park.
