Cambodia, exuberant nature and a lot of history | suoviaggio
Luísa, you published the Travel Guide for Inside and Outside in January 2022, your first book. What motivated you to write this book?
I've read many travel books and felt that something like what I proposed was missing: a publication that brought practical tips and reports on travel experiences, but also provoked reflections with a theoretical basis, including questions of self-knowledge and social and environmental responsibility.
In 2015, when my blog Janelas Abertas (which gave rise to the book) was still only three years old, I started to gather some texts from my travel diaries, from the blog itself and from writing courses that I took over time.
You say you wrote the book you wanted to read when you were 19, when you started traveling. So this book is only aimed at young audiences?
I say that I thought of Luísa from the past in terms of her travel experience. The text is easy to understand and has playful elements, but it is not specifically designed for a young audience.
As many of my readers are people who already followed my work on the blog and on Instagram, I believe that most of my audience is still over 30 years old, like me, and many already travel a lot.
And how was this “journey” of writing the book?
It was very tasty and challenging!
I managed to separate a financial reserve to spend a few months more focused on the book, so it was wonderful to be able to dedicate a good part of my days to a dream.
At the same time, I believe that these are not products that are highly valued in Brazil. If I had turned the same content into an online course, I would have spent much less and could charge four times as much.
Going beyond the book, what was the most transformative experience you've had on a trip?
Doing a master's degree in Spain motivated to say “yes” to all opportunities, interacting intensely with journalists from the Americas, Africa and Asia in an exchange program in Finland, doing an internship in Budapest working with people who are very different from me, living with a riverside family in the Amazon, backpacking through India with an Indian friend and volunteering at a camp with 2.500 youth for two weeks in the German countryside were some of the most life-changing experiences.
When I did an exchange program in Seville, I went to spend a long weekend in Barcelona and they stole my passport with a student visa and, in addition to the financial losses, it was difficult to solve the bureaucracies to be able to travel outside Spain again.
And, of course, I got lost, I spent money for nothing, I stayed in a bad place, I was very cold, I slept in airports… But then everything becomes a story to tell!
Letter to those afraid of traveling alone
Travel even with a churning stomach, hands sweating and people around advising against it. If a part of you says it wants to open up the world, listen to it.
Travel and keep at home the comments of those who think that the whole world is more dangerous than our backyard. Thank those who care for love and care and ignore those who are motivated by control or envy.
That your flight might be cancelled, you might get sick, the destination might not be as expected. That anything can happen, but as long as you don't do anything new, nothing will happen.
Travel not to escape problems, look for the meaning of life or show that it was. But to discover more about yourself and understand that the answers are all in there, not in the mouth of a guru or in a magic formula.
And fear protects us from dangers, but insists on being overprotective. A solo trip is a great opportunity to make it clear that he can accompany you, but only as a passenger: the wheel is yours.
And, of course, some good stories to tell. And on the eve of Carnival, I couldn’t talk to a woman from Recife without asking what her tips are for those who are going to spend Carnival in her homeland…
When you go to Olinda, go out with a group and avoid trying to meet other people there, because it will probably be stressful. If you're willing to face the crowds, look for the more traditional blocks, like Elefante or Eu Acho é Little, which are beautiful; but if you want to take it easy, opt for smaller ones, where it's easier to stay close to the orchestra without so much pressure.
